<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tihama</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tihamah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tihamah is the hot coastal plain along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a> to the Bab el-Mandeb strait. Its southern part, in Saudi Arabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, holds ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizan">Jizan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodeidah">Hodeidah</a> and was farmed and traded across the sea for centuries. In the early 20th century the Idrisid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a> ruled from towns on the plain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tihama.jpg" alt="Tihama" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran_tek-en · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_regions_of_Arabia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name is related to the Proto-Semitic word tihāmat, meaning sea. The same root gave Tiamat, the ancient Mesopotamian god of the sea and of chaos, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> təhōm of Genesis 1:2, meaning the primordial ocean or abyss. In Arabic the word is also taken to mean severe heat and lack of wind, and the temperatures of the plain are probably among the hottest on earth.</p><p>The region is sometimes divided into Tihāmat al-Ḥijāz in the north and Tihāmat ʿAsīr in the south, with the Yemeni part, Tihāmat al-Yaman, as an extension of the latter. The plain is constricted, reaching a width of 60 to 80 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. Yanbu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> and Al Qunfudhah lie in the Hijazi part, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizan">Jizan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodeidah">Hodeidah</a> in the Asiri-Yemeni part. Most of the sandy plain north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> has no trees. In a few places a dense shrub of Vachellia flava grows, and it may be assumed that this was originally the dominant natural vegetation. There are also thickets of Salvadora persica, wild doum palms and planted date palms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations near the village of Al-Mutaynah, led by Edward Keall of the Royal Ontario Museum's Canadian Archaeological Mission, found more than sixteen megalithic menhirs of granite, weighing up to 20 tonnes. Three of the upright stones stood around 2.4 metres tall, and one fallen stone was over 20 metres long. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> tools thought to belong to the same era were dated to around 2400 to 1800 BCE, and an older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tool</a> industry with pottery sherds was dated between 1200 and 800 BCE.</p><p>In the time of the prophet Muhammad many military expeditions took place in Tihamah, among them the Battle of Hamra al-Asad and raids on caravans. From January 623 some of the Muslims raided the Meccan caravans that travelled along the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. At Ḥamra' al-Asad Muhammad made an agreement with Mabad al-Khuzaah of Tihamah. Mabad went to Mecca and exaggerated the force Muhammad had gathered, and Abu Sufyan ibn Harb gave up a planned attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tihamah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The long struggle with Saba and Hadramawt filled the first three centuries CE, and early in the third century Aksumite forces held parts of the western coastal plain, the <b>Tihama</b>, and took part in these wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought off Egyptian expeditions in the 1820s and 1830s and raided the <b>Tihama</b> coast and the Yemeni ports.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-asir">Emirate of Asir</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi, great-grandson of the Moroccan Sufi teacher Ahmad ibn Idris, settled at Sabya in the coastal <b>Tihama</b> of Asir.</blockquote><a href="/state/idrisid-emirate-of-asir">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</a></li><li><blockquote>Ali ibn Mahdi, a preacher from the <b>Tihama</b>, built a following among the tribes of the coastal plain in the 1130s and 1140s, preaching strict morality and condemning the ruling elite.</blockquote><a href="/state/mahdid-dynasty">Mahdid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman Empire returned to Yemen in 1849, occupying the coastal <b>Tihama</b> and Hodeidah.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-vilayet">Yemen Vilayet</a></li><li><blockquote>The Yufirids ruled a tribal highland state from Shibam and Sanaa, a rival to the Ziyadid dynasty in the coastal <b>Tihama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/yufirid-dynasty">Yufirid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
